<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"I know all of it. I've spent the whole night with your complications and my mother's advice and my own hands and the understanding that if I let go of everything, the person who walks out of this tower won't have a single physical thread connecting her to the person who walked in."</div>

<p>I look at her directly.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"I'm not keeping it because I'm afraid to lose it. I'm keeping it because it's MINE. The one piece of this body I'm choosing to carry rather than surrender. My mother said the question isn't what's easier   it's what weight I'm strong enough to bear. This is my weight. I'll bear it."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"Then let's confirm what you're carrying   plainly, once more, so there's no part of it you're agreeing to blind." Her voice returns to something clinical, but gentler than yesterday's first telling. "A fully female body. Breasts, hips, feminine facial structure, the complete exterior presentation. And beneath it: the penis, preserved, though it may change in proportion. A vagina, positioned beneath the retained anatomy. Both systems functional. You will be capable of both penetration and pregnancy."</div>

<p>None of it is new. All of it still lands like stones.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"The court will have questions," she says. "The succession law requires a daughter 'who carries the Mother's form.' Whether your form qualifies will be debated. Your political path will be harder than your brother's."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"It was never going to be easy."</div>

