<p>A pause. The candles burn without flickering. The magic hums.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"Then we proceed." She turns toward the chamber. "The Rite of Becoming   Partial Form."</div>

<p class="path-intro">Behind me, as I start toward the doorway, I hear her say it   quiet, almost to herself, a word I don't fully understand yet.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span><em>"Vael'kirin."</em></div>

<p>I follow her in.</p>

<p>The Ritual Chamber is the same   circle, dome, painted stars, rune-carved altar, four hundred and twelve candles burning steady in enchanted stillness. The same pressure in the air, the same hum in the teeth, the same smell of ozone and beeswax and ancient power.</p>

<p>The difference is in the preparation. Ilara speaks quietly with the four sorceresses. I catch fragments   "partial integration," "dual-channel formation," "preserve the primary structure." The words are technical, precise, the vocabulary of women who manipulate the fundamental architecture of the human body and need language granular enough to describe what they're doing.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"Undress."</div>

<p>The same command. The same grief. I strip   jerkin, shirt, trousers, boots, smallclothes   and each garment removed is a layer of the person I was, and by the time I'm naked I'm standing in the silver light feeling not exposed but offered. Raw material for a sculptor who works in bone and flesh.</p>

